  "o5": {
   "entities": [
    "abilities",
    "tiefling"
   ],
   "answer": "A Tiefling character has these special traits, as described in the source text:\n\n- **Darkvision:** a range of 60 feet.\n- **Fiendish Legacy:** a legacy chosen from the Fiendish Legacies table (Abyssal, Chthonic, or Infernal), which grants the level 1 benefit immediately. The higher-level benefits come at character levels 3 and 5:\n  - **Abyssal:** Resistance to Poison damage and the Poison Spray cantrip (level 1); Ray of Sickness (level 3); Hold Person (level 5).\n  - **Chthonic:** Resistance to Necrotic damage and the Chill Touch cantrip (level 1); False Life (level 3); Ray of Enfeeblement (level 5).\n  - **Infernal:** Resistance to Fire damage and the Fire Bolt cantrip (level 1); Hellish Rebuke (level 3); Darkness (level 5).\n- **Spellcasting:** Intelligence, Wisdom, or Charisma is the spellcasting ability for the legacy's spells, chosen when the legacy is selected.\n- **Otherworldly Presence:** the Thaumaturgy cantrip, which uses the same spellcasting ability as the Fiendish Legacy trait."
  },
  "o6": null,
  "o7": {
   "entities": [
    "proficiency bonus"
   ],
   "answer": "A character's Proficiency Bonus is based on their **total character level**, not their level in a particular class. For example, a level 3 Fighter / level 2 Rogue has the Proficiency Bonus of a level 5 character, which is +3.\n\nYou add your Proficiency Bonus to:\n\n- **Ability checks:** when the GM determines that a skill or tool proficiency is relevant to the check and you have that proficiency. For example, for a Strength (Acrobatics or Athletics) check, you can add it if you have proficiency in Acrobatics or Athletics.\n- **Saving throws:** if you have proficiency in that kind of save.\n- **Attack rolls:** when you attack with a weapon you have proficiency with, or when you attack with a spell."
  },
  "o8": {
   "entities": [
    "ability scores",
    "barbarian"
   ],
   "answer": "Strength"
  },
  "o9": {
   "entities": [
    "fireball"
   ],
   "answer": "Fireball is a level 3 evocation spell (for Sorcerers and Wizards) with a casting time of one action and a range of 150 feet. A bright streak flies from you to a point you choose within range, then bursts into a fiery explosion. Each creature in a 20-foot-radius sphere centered on that point makes a Dexterity saving throw, taking 8d6 fire damage on a failed save or half as much on a success. Flammable objects in the area that aren't being worn or carried start burning. Casting it with a higher-level spell slot increases the damage by 1d6 for each slot level above 3."
  },
  "o10": {
   "entities": [
    "magic missile"
   ],
   "answer": "Each dart deals **2 to 5 Force damage** (1d4 + 1, so a roll of 1 to 4 plus 1)."
  },
  "o11": null,
  "o12": {
   "entities": [
    "mage hand",
    "minor illusion"
   ],
   "answer": "**Mage Hand** creates a spectral, floating hand that physically manipulates objects. It can open unlocked doors or containers, stow or retrieve items from an open container, and pour the contents out of a vial. On later turns, you can control it as a Magic action and move it up to 30 feet. It can't attack, activate magic items, or carry more than 10 pounds. It vanishes if it gets more than 30 feet from you or if you cast the spell again. It has V and S components.\n\n**Minor Illusion** creates a sound or an image instead of a physical object. A sound can range from a whisper to a scream and can be any voice or sound you choose. An image of an object must be no larger than a 5-foot cube and can't create sound, light, smell, or other sensory effects. A creature can use a Study action and a successful Intelligence (Investigation) check against your spell save DC to recognize the illusion, and physical interaction reveals an image to be an illusion because things can pass through it. It has S and M (a bit of fleece) components, and it ends if you cast it again.\n\nBoth spells are Action casting-time cantrips with a 30-foot range and a 1-minute duration, available to Bards, Sorcerers, Warlocks, and Wizards."
  },
  "o13": {
   "entities": [
    "heal"
   ],
   "answer": "According to the source text, the only spell that heals is **Heal** (Level 6 Abjuration, Cleric and Druid spell). It restores 70 Hit Points to a creature you can see within 60 feet and also ends the Blinded, Deafened, and Poisoned conditions on that target. When cast with a higher-level spell slot, the healing increases by 10 for each spell slot level above 6.\n\nThe source text doesn't describe any other spells, so it doesn't say whether other spells can heal."
  },
